#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096340 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b194bf2f-feaa-5656-a3ee-0ff466a39e77" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing a Trigonometric Function"}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d7a77a89-0535-54da-a246-36ee5b4e9e7f" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3634" :question/problem "Differentiate $x\\cos x$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=x$ and\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\cos x\n$$\n\nSo, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} (x\\cos x)' &= u'(x) \\cdot v(x) + u(x) \\cdot v'(x) \\\\ &= (x)'\\cdot \\cos x + x\\cdot (\\cos x)' \\\\ & = 1 \\cdot \\cos x + x (-\\sin x) \\\\ & = \\cos x - x \\sin x \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096341 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f15a60dd-f6a7-577d-bdbc-148032de94c8" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing an Exponential or Logarithmic Function"}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6bb0eded-7277-5ab5-8c13-bfc480403b1a" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3635" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=x^2\\ln x$, find $f'(x)$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x) = x^2$ and $v(x)=\\ln x$. So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{\\prime }(x) &= (x^{2})^{\\prime }\\ln x + x^{2}(\\ln x)^{\\prime } \\\\\n&= 2x \\cdot \\ln x + x^{2}(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\\n&= 2x\\ln x + x \\\\\n&= x(2\\ln x + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096342 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee64135e-9b9f-5429-a4df-efa522042b83" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Product  at a Given Point"}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "072ce609-b04d-57b0-9c7d-cbb8765c38fa" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3636" :question/problem "If a curve $\\mathcal C$ is given by $y=e^x\\sin x$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\dfrac \\pi 2$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x) = e^x$ and\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sin{x}\n$$\n\nThen, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = e^x,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos x\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= u\\frac{dv}{dx} + v\\frac{du}{dx} \\\\\n&= e^{x} \\cdot \\cos x + \\sin x \\cdot e^{x} \\\\\n&= e^{x}(\\cos x + \\sin x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac\\pi 2\n$$\n\ninto the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{x = \\pi /2} &= e^{\\pi /2}(\\cos (\\frac{\\pi }{2}) + \\sin (\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\\n&= e^{\\pi /2}(0 + 1) \\\\\n&= e^{\\pi /2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $e^{\\pi/2}$."}]}
